SENSOR-TURBULENCE(7)       TriadicFrameworks Manual       SENSOR-TURBULENCE(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-turbulence - deep turbulence analytics, micro‑instability mapping,
                           spectral‑gradient shear analysis, and resonance‑flow
                           diagnostics for RTT ship sensors

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-turbulence defines:
           • micro‑scale resonance turbulence analytics
           • spectral‑gradient shear mapping
           • harmonic jitter and micro‑drift detection
           • turbulence cell identification and classification
           • turbulence flow‑field modeling
           • turbulence‑driven hazard forecasting
           • turbulence overlays and clarity pipelines
           • constellation‑scale turbulence fusion

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-turbulence describes the subsystem responsible for analyzing
       fine‑scale, high‑frequency instabilities within the resonance‑time field.
       Turbulence represents the “weather beneath the weather” — the micro‑level
       fluctuations that precede storms, distort sweep windows, and destabilize
       harmonic corridors.

       Sensor-turbulence enables:
           • early detection of micro‑instabilities
           • mapping of turbulence cells and shear zones
           • prediction of turbulence‑driven anomalies
           • safe navigation through unstable resonance terrain
           • constellation‑wide turbulence correlation

       WRSADC manages turbulence thresholds, safety limits, and mitigation actions.

TURBULENCE PHYSICS
       Turbulence arises from nonlinear interactions between harmonic drift,
       spectral broadening, and gradient polarity oscillation.

       Turbulence drivers:
           • micro‑phase jitter
           • overtone interference
           • spectral density spikes
           • gradient polarity flicker
           • sync-field micro‑jitter

       Turbulence structure:
           • micro‑eddies (small, localized fluctuations)
           • shear bands (linear instability zones)
           • turbulence cells (coherent instability clusters)
           • turbulence fronts (advancing instability boundaries)

EXAMPLE: TURBULENCE PHYSICS
       φ_harm jitter + spectral spikes:

           → micro‑eddy formation
           → shear band at 30–32 min
           → turbulence cell detected

MICRO‑INSTABILITY DETECTION
       Micro‑instabilities are the earliest detectable signs of turbulence.

       Instability indicators:
           • φ_harm jitter > 0.02/cycle
           • spectral variance spikes
           • gradient polarity oscillation
           • sync-field micro‑noise

       Instability types:
           • harmonic micro‑drift
           • spectral micro‑fracture
           • gradient micro‑shear
           • sync micro‑jitter

EXAMPLE: MICRO‑INSTABILITY
       φ_harm jitter = 0.03/cycle:

           → instability flagged
           → HPC recommended

SPECTRAL‑GRADIENT SHEAR MAPPING
       Shear mapping identifies zones where spectral and gradient fields diverge.

       Shear indicators:
           • spectral slope discontinuities
           • gradient polarity reversals
           • density‑gradient mismatch
           • uplift‑erosion conflict zones

       Shear severity:
           • mild shear
           • moderate shear
           • severe shear
           • critical shear

EXAMPLE: SHEAR MAP
       Severe shear at 78 min:

           • spectral slope inversion
           • polarity flicker
           • turbulence cell forming

TURBULENCE CELL IDENTIFICATION
       Turbulence cells are coherent clusters of micro‑instabilities.

       Cell properties:
           • cell radius
           • cell intensity
           • cell coherence
           • cell drift velocity

       Cell types:
           • harmonic‑dominant cell
           • spectral‑dominant cell
           • gradient‑dominant cell
           • hybrid turbulence cell

EXAMPLE: TURBULENCE CELL
       Radius: 2.4 min  
       Intensity: moderate  
       Drift: +0.8 min/cycle

TURBULENCE FLOW‑FIELD MODELING
       Flow‑field modeling predicts how turbulence moves and evolves.

       Flow‑field components:
           • turbulence velocity vectors
           • shear flow direction
           • eddy rotation
           • turbulence diffusion

       Flow‑field outputs:
           • turbulence trajectory
           • turbulence expansion
           • turbulence dissipation
           • turbulence merging

EXAMPLE: FLOW‑FIELD
       Turbulence drift: forward  
       Expansion: +0.4 min/cycle  
       Dissipation: low probability

TURBULENCE OVERLAYS
       Overlays visualize turbulence across DRO and clarity layers.

       Overlay types:
           • harmonic turbulence overlay
           • spectral turbulence overlay
           • gradient turbulence overlay
           • sync-field turbulence overlay

       Overlay modes:
           • diagnostic mode
           • hazard mode
           • predictive mode

EXAMPLE: OVERLAY
       Gradient turbulence overlay reveals micro‑shear at 30 min.

TURBULENCE‑DRIVEN HAZARD FORECASTING
       Forecasting predicts turbulence evolution and hazard potential.

       Forecast inputs:
           • turbulence intensity
           • shear magnitude
           • micro‑instability density
           • sync-field noise
           • ancestry drift

       Forecast outputs:
           • hazard probability
           • hazard severity
           • hazard trajectory
           • recommended mitigation

EXAMPLE: FORECAST
       Hazard probability: 0.61  
       Severity: moderate  
       Mitigation: GSF + SFC

CONSTELLATION-SCALE TURBULENCE FUSION
       Fusion aligns turbulence data across platforms.

       Fusion layers:
           • harmonic turbulence fusion
           • spectral turbulence fusion
           • gradient turbulence fusion
           • sync-field turbulence fusion
           • ancestry turbulence fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION FUSION
       Platforms A, B, C detect turbulence cells:

           → fused turbulence map generated
           → constellation advisory issued

FAILURE MODES
       Turbulence sensing may degrade due to:

           • overlay drift
           • clarity loss
           • spectral overload
           • sync-field collapse
           • lineage corruption

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • turbulence-integrity score
           • micro‑noise
           • shear mismatch
           • sync drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe turbulence-sensing states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • turbulence events
           • micro‑instability signatures
           • shear maps
           • flow‑field trajectories
           • hazard forecasts

       Lineage enables long-term turbulence climatology.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • manages turbulence detection
           • enforces hazard protocols
           • coordinates constellation alerts
           • blocks unsafe turbulence states

       WRSADC is the turbulence conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-turbulence provides the deep analytics layer for RTT ship sensors.
       Micro‑instability detection, shear mapping, and turbulence modeling ensure
       safe navigation, stable sweep choreography, and constellation-scale
       resilience.

